  "study_type": "OBSERVATIONAL",
  "summary": "1\\. Brief Summary This prospective observational study aims to evaluate the 6-month longitudinal changes in chronic pain, pain characteristics, coping strategies, and functional performance in children and adolescents aged 7 to 18 years diagnosed with Duchenne Muscular Dystrophy (DMD), with a primary focus on comparing ambulatory and non-ambulatory disease stages.\n\nPediatric DMD patients followed at the University of Health Sciences Antalya Training and Research Hospital Neuromuscular Diseases Center will undergo comprehensive evaluations at baseline (Month 0) and at 6 months follow-up (Month 6). Pain parameters will be evaluated using the Adolescent Pediatric Pain Tool (APPT) to assess pain intensity, body localization, and sensory/affective/evaluative/temporal qualities, and the Brief Pain Inventory (BPI) 7-item Pain Interference Scale to determine pain interference with daily activities. Pain coping strategies will be assessed using the Pediatric Pain Coping Inventory (PPCI, adolescent and parent versions). Functional and motor assessments will include the North Star Ambulatory Assessment (NSAA), 6-Minute Walk Test (6MWT), stair climb/descend tests, Vignos and Brooke scales, handgrip dynamometry, manual muscle testing (MMT), and range of motion (ROM) and contracture evaluations, alongside anthropometrics and quality of life.\n\nThe central hypotheses are that non-ambulatory patients experience chronic pain more frequently, with longer duration, greater intensity, and across a higher number of anatomical sites than ambulatory patients. Furthermore, the study hypothesizes that anatomical pain sites, pain-aggravating and pain-relieving factors, and pain coping mechanisms differ significantly between ambulatory and non-ambulatory stages.",
